\begin{slide}
Kalman Filter Theory vs. Reality
\begin{itemize}
\item Kalman theory based on multiple assumptions
\begin{itemize}
\item All states are modeled correctly
\item Noise parameters are accurately known
\item Initial values are appropriate
\item Process and measurement noise are both Gaussian
\item Process noise and measurement noise are uncorrelated
\item Measurements are taken at regularly scheduled intervals
\end{itemize} 
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Kalman Filter Theory vs. Reality
\begin{itemize}
  \item How do you select appropriate sensors for the problem at hand?
  \item Some obvious general rules:
  \begin{itemize}
	\item the lower the sensor noise characteristics, the more accurate the estimates (narrow sensor noise variance)
    \item the more the merrier, i.e., the more sensors the better
    \item maximize the number of directly observable states
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Kalman Filter Theory vs. Reality
\begin{itemize}
  \item Some less obvious general rules:
  \begin{itemize}
	\item short-term / long-term complementarity (e.g, GPS vs. INS)
    \item frequency response of sensor should match that of system
    \item noise spectrum of sensor should be maximally disjoint from that of system
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
\includegraphics{day3_seg1_numerical/sensor_selection.eps}
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Kalman Filter Theory vs. Reality
\begin{itemize}
  \item Choose sensors to match states.\\
E.g., consider the state model
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
\]
with the measurement model
\[
z = \begin{bmatrix} 1\;\; 0 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
+ v
\]
Here $x_1$ is observable but $x_2$ is not.
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Kalman Filter Theory vs. Reality: Sensor Selection
 \begin{itemize}
  \item Examine the $H$ matrix that you would use with this sensor!
  \item If it renders some states unobservable, then consider adding additional sensors for those states, or replacing this sensor with one that is more favorable.
 \end{itemize}
\end{slide}